WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.098
A reader needs 100 milliseconds to read each settlement line.

2
00:00:07.098 --> 00:00:10.676
A ledger writer needs 300 milliseconds to post it.

3
00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:12.823
A plain flow connects the two.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.542
The reader emits the first line at 100 milliseconds.

5
00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:18.517
When does it emit the second?

6
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:24.023
Picture one cook and one diner.

7
00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:27.797
collect runs the flow block, and that block is the cook.

8
00:00:27.797 --> 00:00:30.359
Reading a line takes 100 milliseconds.

9
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:34.187
Then the cook calls emit, and walks the plate to the table.

10
00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:37.159
emit runs your collector's code right inside it.

11
00:00:37.159 --> 00:00:38.212
That's the diner.

12
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.644
The diner posts to the ledger for 300 milliseconds.

13
00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:46.605
And the cook stands at the table the whole time, because emit hasn't returned.

14
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.116
emit returns only when the box inside it closes, at 400.

15
00:00:51.116 --> 00:00:55.432
Then the cook reads the next line, which takes 100 more.

16
00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:58.361
So the second emit is at 500, not 200.

17
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:01.306
The same happens for every line.

18
00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:03.732
Each one costs 100 plus 300.

19
00:01:03.732 --> 00:01:09.711
Three lines take 1200 milliseconds, where the writer alone needs 900.

20
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:12.383
Two rules come out of this one picture.

21
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:14.189
First, a flow is sequential.

22
00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:20.447
While emit(1) is open, the cook can't start line 2, and the diner never holds two plates at once.

23
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.523
Second, a slow collector slows the producer.

24
00:01:23.523 --> 00:01:26.977
The reader could read a line every 100 milliseconds.

25
00:01:26.977 --> 00:01:30.897
Here it reads one every 400, because it waits at the table.

26
00:01:30.897 --> 00:01:34.152
That is backpressure, for free, with no protocol.

27
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:36.340
One thing the picture gets wrong.

28
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.353
The waiting cook is a suspended coroutine, and it holds no thread.

29
00:01:40.353 --> 00:01:44.913
While your collector waits on the ledger, the thread runs other coroutines.

30
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.888
In code, nothing marks the wait.

31
00:01:47.888 --> 00:01:54.347
Kotlin's emit and Java's accept are plain calls, and they return when the collector's code returns.

32
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.987
So emit is a direct call into the collector.

33
00:01:57.987 --> 00:02:02.656
A plain flow handles one value at a time, at the speed of its collector.

34
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:03.668
Quick check.

35
00:02:03.668 --> 00:02:08.444
A reader needs 50 milliseconds per line, and the writer needs 200.

36
00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:10.180
When is the second emit?
